- TODO: statistical review of Chapter 14 - Novelty effects
    - Date: 2026-04-30
    - Summary
        - Chapter walks three measurement schemes (calendar day, days-since-exposure, exposure-count) over a single simulated event log and contrasts decay, primacy, U-shape patterns. Two-lens commentary names a Bayesian hierarchical model as the honest framing. Review surfaced five gaps where the chapter glosses over confounds and survival biases that the same simulator already produces, plus a missing pointer to the principled parametric fix.
        - Critic and defender iterated twice. All five items below survived round two as concrete, in-scope edits. Out-of-scope items (full PyMC fit, formal selection-bias derivation) deferred.
    - Items
        - [APPLIED] Loop B, calendar-day confound: rewrite the bullet to say the issue is that each calendar day is a mixture of users at different per-user exposure-tenures, not calendar time itself. Current phrasing "confounds calendar effects with user-tenure effects" is true but vague; reader cannot tell why averaging across the cohort's tenure distribution shifts the curve.
            - File: chapters/14-novelty/chapter.log line 20, propagate to chapter.md line 25
            - Suggested phrasing: "Calendar-day is what dashboards typically show. Each calendar day is a mixture of users at different per-user exposure-tenures (early arrivers are deep into decay, late arrivers are still on day-1 of exposure). That mixture, not calendar time, is what shifts the curve."
        - [APPLIED] Loop B, days-since-exposure survival caveat: add a sentence that late-arriving users cannot contribute to the long-exposure tail, so the right side of the curve is an early-arriver subsample. With 40 days and arrivals throughout, the day-30 exposure bucket is populated only by users whose first exposure was in the first ~10 calendar days. If those users differ systematically (eager early adopters), the tail mis-estimates the asymptote.
            - File: chapters/14-novelty/chapter.log lines 21-22, propagate to chapter.md
        - [APPLIED] Loop B, exposure-count heavy-user weighting: add a sentence that exposure-count buckets are weighted by visit frequency. arrival_rate=0.4 in the simulator is uniform across users, so the simulator does not exhibit this, but the chapter should warn that in real data heavy users dominate high-count buckets, and habituation curves estimated this way conflate "users habituate" with "frequent users had a different baseline lift to begin with."
            - File: chapters/14-novelty/chapter.log line 22, propagate to chapter.md
        - [APPLIED] Loop C framing: reframe the three shapes as illustrative, not exhaustive or canonical. Add one sentence noting that real populations are usually mixtures of users with different shapes, so a population-mean curve can be flat while individuals exhibit strong individual decay or ramp.
            - File: chapters/14-novelty/chapter.log lines 26-32, propagate to chapter.md lines 31-39
        - [APPLIED] Two-lens commentary, name the parametric fix explicitly: the Bayesian bullet describes a hierarchical model with latent decay shape but never names it. Add the explicit name "fit an exponential decay with per-user random effects on initial lift and asymptote", and note the frequentist analogue is nonlinear least squares with the same random-effects structure (lme4-style). This gives the reader a search term and clarifies that both lenses agree on the structural model, just disagree on inference.
            - File: chapters/14-novelty/chapter.log lines 34-36, propagate to chapter.md lines 43-45
    - Out of scope
